think The fallacy to kill in the docs: never "it's immutable, so we cannot debride it" -- always "it's default-immutable, so it takes a circled debride, which then re-seals or re-makes what it touched"
think Two stale claims to correct while here: the old rule said a deep debride unavoidably unsigns commits (disproven 20260817 -- the urbit reroot re-signed 2901 via filter-branch commit-tree -S), and it cited the retired work-in-progress/CAIRNS.md path
obs Added an Accrete-sometimes-breach section to .claude/rules/debride.md (and the cursor twin): accrete is the default, breach/debride the sanctioned exceptions; immutability is a lock and debride is the key; re-signing is part of the remake, not a lost cost; only the re-clone is unavoidable
